SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Proctor v. Sappi North America
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE  APPELLATE DIVISION \nWORKERS’ COMPENSATION B
 OARD  Case No. App. Div. 25-0008 \n  Decision No. 26-01 \n \n \nPHILIP PRO
 CTOR \n(Appellant) \n \nv. \nSAPPI NORTH AMERICA\, INC. \n(Appellee/Self-I
 nsured) \n \n \nArgued: September 23\, 2025 \nDecided: February 25\, 2026 
 \n \nPanel Members:  Administrative Law Judges Chabot\, Sands\, and Smith 
 \nBy:  Administrative Law Judge Sands \n \n[¶1]  Philip Proctor appeals f
 rom a decision of a Workers’ Compensation \nBoard administrative law jud
 ge ( Stovall\, ALJ ) denying \, in part\, his Petition for \nPenalties. Th
 rough his Petition\, Mr. Proctor sought to establish that  Sappi North \nA
 merica\, Inc. \, violated the Workers’ Compensation Act by taking an off
 set for \npension benefits distributed by Sappi and subsequently placed by
  Mr. Proctor into \nan Individual Retirement Account (IRA). See 39-A M.R.S
 .A . § 221. 1 The ALJ \ndetermined that Sappi is entitled to take the off
 set and declined to impose penalties \nbeyond those associated with late p
 ayments under 39-A M.R.S.A. § 205(3). We find \nno error and affirm the d
 ecision.   \n \n \n  1  Because this appeal pertains  to whether Sappi has
  a right under section 221 of the Workers’ \nCompensation Act to take an
  offset for pension benefits paid\, and does not address the imposition or
  denial \nof penalties\, the Appellate Division\, rather than the Superior
  Court\, has jurisdiction to entertain this appeal. \nSee 39-A M.R.S.A §
 § 318\, 32-B\, and 360. \n \n2 \n \nI.  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Mr. Proctor s
 ustained a left-hand crush injury on April 28\, 2010\, while \nworking for
  Sappi . Ultimately\, he was  unable to return to work\, and Sappi has \nv
 oluntarily paid him indemnity benefits since.   \n[¶3]  Through his emplo
 yment at Sappi\, Mr. Proctor participated in a defined \nbenefit pension p
 lan. Pursuant to the plan’s terms\, benefits must be distributed when \n
 individuals no longer employed by Sappi reach age 65 . Notice is sent to t
 hose \nindividuals in advance with three options for distribution. The def
 ault distribution\, \nper t\nhttps://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appella
 te/2026decisions/26-01_Proctor_v._Sappi_%20N._America_2-25-26.pdf
URL:https://www.maine.gov/wcb/Departments/appellate/2026decisions/26-01_Pr
 octor_v._Sappi_%20N._America_2-25-26.pdf
